WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:12.077
In Spain, 1,119 people died on interurban roads in 2025. Most of them on roads with one lane in each direction, little traffic and poor coverage.

2
00:00:12.427 --> 00:00:23.627
SENDA travels with you: it warns you of what you can't see, calls for help even with no network and guides you if you stop to help. SENDA never replaces 112, or your attention on the road.

3
00:00:25.227 --> 00:00:36.001
Fog, 120 km/h and 1.2 seconds between cars. Each driver reacts to the brake lights ahead and the delays add up: five cars crash in a chain.

4
00:00:36.351 --> 00:00:48.084
With SENDA, the first car's phone detects the braking from its GPS speed and warns everyone at once. Only the car tailgating right behind still crashes: only a safe distance fixes that.

5
00:00:49.684 --> 00:00:59.967
A night-time crash on 18 kilometres of road with no network at all. The crashed car's phone broadcasts the SOS, and any passing car with SENDA picks it up and carries it.

6
00:01:00.317 --> 00:01:10.237
In this simulation, with only one car in ten running SENDA, the alert gets out in about seven minutes. And oncoming cars warn the drivers heading towards the crash.

7
00:01:11.837 --> 00:01:18.535
GPS works without the Internet. What's missing is knowing what to say: road, kilometre point and direction.

8
00:01:18.885 --> 00:01:29.104
SENDA works it out with no network, adds the nearest visible landmark, such as a wind turbine, and keeps tracking you inside a tunnel. The road shown is invented, for the demo.

9
00:01:30.704 --> 00:01:39.109
Whoever stops can coordinate the scene using the DGT's routine: protect, alert and help. Each task is confirmed by another person.

10
00:01:39.459 --> 00:01:47.843
When the Guardia Civil arrives, it takes command and receives the summary. Confirmed actions earn civic points in a record that cannot be altered.

11
00:01:49.443 --> 00:01:57.699
The first to know is the phone of the driver who made the mistake: three positions in a row against the direction of the carriageway are enough to warn them.

12
00:01:58.049 --> 00:02:04.172
Oncoming drivers get the time left before they meet and what to do: keep to the right-hand lane and slow down.

13
00:02:05.772 --> 00:02:11.767
The end of a traffic jam grows backwards. Using traffic-flow theory, SENDA calculates how fast.

14
00:02:12.117 --> 00:02:17.493
So it warns you where the queue will be when you get there, not where it was, and you slow down much earlier.

15
00:02:19.093 --> 00:02:25.322
SENDA only uses public cameras released for reuse, and only to measure the state of the road, never people.

16
00:02:25.672 --> 00:02:36.445
With a dark object at a known distance, it works out the visibility in metres and the speed at which you can stop within what you can see. This is a synthetic image, made to test the method.

17
00:02:38.045 --> 00:02:46.216
112 and DATEX II traffic information are common across the whole European Union. That is why the same core works in the Netherlands.

18
00:02:46.566 --> 00:02:53.158
There, a red cross over a lane means the lane is closed. SENDA reads it from open data and tells you in Dutch.

19
00:02:54.758 --> 00:03:06.235
Speed radars, message signs and traffic counting stations are not radios we can use: each one talks to the DGT over a closed channel. Detecting speed radars is also prohibited.

20
00:03:06.585 --> 00:03:15.567
SENDA adds up what the DGT publishes. In this example, readings from the counting stations show where the queue starts and how fast it grows.

21
00:03:17.167 --> 00:03:22.969
Everything you have seen is calculated by the app's own core, with automated tests that run offline.

22
00:03:23.319 --> 00:03:31.277
The most important part is still missing: trials on real roads, the native app for car-to-car alerts and agreements with the DGT.

23
00:03:32.877 --> 00:03:37.250
SENDA: the channel that remains when everything else fails. Try it on the web.
